\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on at least one of the three lines.  \nA line is called **sunny** if it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad \n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad \n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}) .\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line cannot contain two points of \\(P\\) that share the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate,\nthe same \\(y\\)‑coordinate, or the same value of \\(x+y\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Possible non‑sunny lines\n\nA non‑sunny line must be of one of the three forms above.  \nAmong the points of \\(P\\) the only values that occur are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{horizontal }y=c & c=1,2,3\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{vertical }x=c   & c=1,2,3\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{diagonal }x+y=c & c=2,3,4 .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIf a non‑sunny line contains only a single point of \\(P\\), the remaining five points\nmust be covered by the two sunny lines; this is clearly impossible because each\nsunny line can contain at most three points of \\(P\\) (one from each forbidden\nfamily) and \\(2\\cdot3<5\\).  \nConsequently the non‑sunny line must contain **at least two** points of \\(P\\).\nUp to symmetry the only such lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y=1,\\; y=2,\\; x=1,\\; x=2,\\; x+y=3,\\; x+y=4 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Examination of each case  \n\nFor each candidate non‑sunny line \\(L_{0}\\) we denote by  \n\n\\[\nR = P\\setminus L_{0}\n\\]\n\nthe set of points that are not on \\(L_{0}\\).  \nWe shall show that for every listed \\(L_{0}\\) the set \\(R\\) contains three points\nsuch that **any** line joining two of them is prohibited; therefore two sunny\nlines cannot cover all points of \\(R\\).\n\n---\n\n| \\(L_{0}\\) | Points on \\(L_{0}\\) | \\(R\\) (remaining points) | Forbidden pairs in \\(R\\