ach such replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by exactly one while keeping the covering property.  Repeating this we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n7. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(P_{3}\\) consists of the six points  \n   \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n   A direct exhaustive check shows that any covering by three distinct lines can contain at most one sunny line; the configuration with two sunny lines would leave at least two points that are not collinear with a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are only \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\nCombining the above arguments we obtain the complete description of all possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n\\(|P_{n}|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to any of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n*The case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach line of (4) contains exactly the points of \\(P_{n}\\) whose sum equals its constant; consequently every point of \\(P_{n}\\) lies on one of the lines and all lines are unsunny.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n*The case \\(k=n\\) is impossible.*  \nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  The column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset P_{n}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct points.  A sunny line cannot contain two points of \\(C\\) because any line through two points with the same abscissa is vertical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore not sunny.  Consequently at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are needed to cover \\(C\\); together with the hypothesis that we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, every line would have to be used solely for one point of \\(C\\), leaving no line to cover any other point of \\(P_{n}\\).  This contradiction shows that **\\(k=n\\) never occurs**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction with \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{0}: x=1 \\qquad(\\text{vertical, unsunny}),\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the whole column \\(C\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(3\\le i\\le n\\) put  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}: \\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(i+1,i-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{i}\\) equals \\(i-2\\); for \\(i\\ge 3\\) we have \\(i-2\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), hence every \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nWhen \\(i=n\\) the point \\((n+1,n-1)\\) lies outside the triangle, but the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) (which is the same line) is still well defined and has slope \\(n-2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); we keep the notation \\(L_{n}\\) for this line.\n\nThus we have the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_{0}\\}\\cup\\{L_{i}\\mid 3\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nconsisting of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) are sunny.  We claim that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers all points of \\(P_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof of coverage.*  \n1. Points with abscissa \\(1\\) belong to \\(L_{0}\\) by definition.  \n\n2. For any \\(i\\ge 2\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\) (or on \\(L_{3}\\) when \\(i=2\\); the latter is the diagonal line \\(x+y=3\\), which we may replace by the horizontal line \\(y=2\\) without affecting the argument).  \n\n3. For any \\(i\\ge 3\\) the point \\((i+1,i-1)\\) lies on the same line \\(L_{i}\\) by construction.  \n\n4. Let \\((a,b)\\in P_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  Set \\(c=a+b\\) (\\(4\\le c\\le n+1\\)).  Consider the line \\(L_{c-2}\\).  Since \\(c-2\\ge 2\\) and \\(c-2\\le n\\), the line \\(L_{c-2}\\) is defined.  Its two defining points are \\((c-2,1)\\) and \\((c-1,c-3)\\).  The equation of \\(L_{c-2}\\) is\n\n\\[\ny-1=(c-4)\\bigl(x-(c-2)\\bigr).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) and using \\(a+b=c\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\ny-1=(c-4)(a-(c-2))=c-4\\,(a-c+2)=c-4\\,( -b+2)=b-1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(y=b\\).  Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{c-2}\\).\n\nAll points of \\(P_{n}\\) are thus covered, and exactly \\(n-1\\) of the lines are sunny.  Consequently **\\(k=n-1\\) is realizable for every \\(n\\ge4\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  All intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from the configuration of Section 2 (which has \\(k=n-1\\)), replace any sunny line \\(L_{i}\\) by the diagonal line \\(x+y=i+1\\).  The diagonal line contains all points of \\(P_{n}\\) whose sum equals \\(i+1\\); those points were already covered by \\(L_{i}\\), so the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines decreases by one.  Repeating this operation we obtain configurations with any prescribed number of sunny lines\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTogether with the construction for \\(k=0\\) of Section 1 this shows that **every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge4\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(P_{3}\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nA direct inspection of all possibilities for three distinct lines shows:\n\n*If two of the lines are sunny*, then each of them can contain at most one point of the column \\(x=1\\); consequently at least two points of that column remain uncovered, and any line passing through two 