{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   As argued in step 2, any admissible configuration must contain at least two unsunny lines, so \\(k\\le1\\).  \n   The three diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) give \\(k=0\\); the three lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad x+y=3,\\qquad\\text{the line through }(2,2)\\text{ and }(3,1)\n   \\]\n   give \\(k=1\\).  Hence for \\(n=3\\) the possible values are exactly \\(k=0,1\\).\n\nCombining the above arguments yields the complete description of all admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to any line of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary lower bound on the number of unsunny lines\n\nThe column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset P_{n}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points.  A sunny line can intersect \\(C\\) in at most one point, for a line meeting \\(C\\) in two distinct points would be vertical and therefore belong to \\(\\mathcal V\\).  Consequently at least one unsunny line is required to cover all points of \\(C\\); we may assume that the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is present.\n\nThe points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) have sum \\(3\\).  The unique line through them has slope \\(-1\\) and belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\); hence it is unsunny.  Thus **any** family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(P_{n}\\) must contain **at least two** unsunny lines.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration attaining the bound \\(k=n-2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nDefine the following lines.\