unny line \\(\\lambda_t\\). The point \\((1,t)\\) is still covered (now by \\(\\lambda_t\\)); every other point of the former row \\(y=t\\) has the form \\((a,t)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and is covered by the still\u2011present horizontal line \\(y=t-1\\) because \\(a+(t-1)\\le n+1\\). Thus the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\n   Repeating this replacement for rows \\(t=n,n-1,\\dots ,n-k+1\\) yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines. The construction works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The two extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are realizable, and Lemma\u202f2 together with the iterative replacement described above shows that every intermediate integer \\(k\\) can be obtained. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_n\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The configuration with \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=-\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+\\frac{n+2}{i}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\); it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+2-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\;n+1-i)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(S_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+(n+2-i)=n+2\\le n+1\\;(\\text{equality holds only for }i=1),\n\\qquad\n(i+1)+(n+1-i)=n+2\\le n+1\\;(\\text{again equality only for }i=n).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the coordinates of \\(P_i\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nn+2-i=-\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,i+\\frac{n+2}{i}=-(i-1)+\\frac{n+2}{i},\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to an identity; similarly \\(Q_i\\) satisfies (1).  \nHence \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through both \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\):\ngiven \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) set \\(i=a\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+2-a-1\\).  Consequently either \\(b=n+2-a\\) (the point is \\(P_a\\)) or \\(b=n+1-a\\) (the point is \\(Q_{a-1}\\)); in both cases the point belongs to \\(\\ell_a\\).  Thus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_n\\) and consists of exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  From \\(k\\) sunny lines to \\(k+1\\) sunny lines  \n\nAssume we already have a covering consisting of  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n* \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\),\n\nand that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  \nWe show how to replace the horizontal line \\(y=n-k\\) by a sunny line while keeping a covering; this increases the number of sunny lines by one.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,\\;n-k),\\qquad\nC=(n-k+1,\\;n-k-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(S_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\n1+(n-k)=n-k+1\\le n+1,\\qquad\n(n-k+1)+(n-k-1)=2n-2k\\le n+1\\;(\\text{since }k\\ge0).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\lambda\\) through \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{(n-k-1)-(n-k)}{(n-k+1)-1}\n   =-\\frac{1}{\\,n-k\\,},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\lambda\\) is sunny.\n\nNow delete the horizontal line \\(y=n-k\\) and insert \\(\\lambda\\).  \nThe point \\(A\\) is still covered (by \\(\\lambda\\)).  \nEvery other point \\((a,n-k)\\) of the deleted row satisfies \\(a\\ge2\\); such a point also belongs to the still\u2011present horizontal line \\(y=n-k-1\\) because  \n\n\\[\na+(n-k-1)\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus all points formerly covered by \\(y=n-k\\) remain covered, and no other point is lost.  Consequently we obtain a covering with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines and \\(n-(k+1)\\) horizontal lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Obtaining all values of \\(k\\)\n\nStart with the configuration of Section\u202f1 (\\(k=0\\)).  \nApply the operation of Section\u202f3 repeatedly.  \nAfter the first application we have \\(k=1\\); after the second, \\(k=2\\); \u2026 after the \\(k\\)-th application we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines.  \nSince the operation can be performed as long as a horizontal line remains, we can reach every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Exhaustiveness  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for the extreme values \\(k=0\\) (Section\u202f1) and \\(k=n\\) (Section\u202f2).  \nSection\u202f3 shows that from any admissible configuration we can increase the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.  \nConsequently every integer between the extremes is also attainable.  \nNo further restriction on \\(k\\) exists, because the construction works for **every** \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
