can find another construction.\n\nLet's think about covering the triangular lattice with n lines, all sunny. For n=3, we found a construction. For n=4, we haven't found one yet. Let's try to find a construction for n=4 manually.\n\nWe need 4 sunny lines covering all 10 points.\n\nWe can try to use lines that each cover 3 points, if possible. For n=4, we need total coverage of 10 points. If we have three lines covering 3 points each (total 9) and one line covering at least 1 point, we can cover all. Or we could have two lines covering 3 points each, and two lines covering 2 points each (total 10). So we need to find lines that cover 3 points.\n\nWhich lines can cover 3 points in S_4? Let's search.\n\nConsider line y = -x + c (slope -1) not allowed. So we cannot have lines with slope -1.\n\nConsider line y = x + c (slope 1). For integer points, we need points where b - a = c. For S_4, possible differences: b - a can be negative or positive. Let's compute possible differences for points in S_4:\n\nPoints:\n(1,1): diff 0\n(1,2): diff 1\n(1,3): diff 2\n(1,4): diff 3\n(2,1): diff -1\n(2,2): diff 0\n(2,3): diff 1\n(3,1): diff -2\n(3,2): diff -1\n(4,1): diff -3\n\nSo differences range from -3 to 3. So lines y = x + c for c = -3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3. Each such line passes through points with that difference.\n\nLet's see how many points each line has:\n\n- c = -3: points where b = a - 3 => (4,1) only.\n- c = -2: (3,1) only.\n- c = -1: (2,1),(3,2) => 2 points.\n- c = 0: (1,1),(2,2) => 2 points.\n- c = 1: (1,2),(2,3) => 2 points.\n- c = 2: (1,3) only? (2,4) not in S_4. So (1,3) only.\n- c = 3: (1,4) only.\n\nThus lines with slope 1 can cover at most 2 points (except maybe c = -1,0,1 which have 2). So they cannot cover 3 points.\n\nConsider lines with slope 2: y = 2x + c. For integer points, b = 2a + c. Let's see possible a values 1..4, b must be ≤ n+1 - a = 5 - a. So b ≤ 5 - a. For a=1, b ≤ 4. So b = 2*1 + c = 2 + c ≤ 4 => c ≤ 2. Also b ≥ 1 => 2 + c ≥ 1 => c ≥ -1. So c ∈ [-1,2] integer. For a=2, b = 4 + c ≤ 3 => c ≤ -1. Also b ≥ 1 => 4 + c ≥ 1 => c ≥ -3. So c ∈ [-3,-1]. Intersection of c ranges for a=1 and a=2: c ∈ [-1,-1] => c = -1. So line y = 2x - 1 passes through (1,1) and (2,3). Check a=3: b = 6 - 1 = 5 > 2 (since b ≤ 2?), not allowed. So line y = 2x - 1 covers (1,1) and (2,3). That's 2 points.\n\nLine y = 2x + 0: (1,2) (b=2) okay, (2,4) not allowed. So only (1,2). So not many.\n\nLine y = 2x + 1: (1,3) okay, (2,5) not allowed. So only (1,3).\n\nLine y = 2x - 2: (1,0) not allowed (b≥1). So no.\n\nThus slope 2 lines cover at most 2 points.\n\nConsider slope -2: y = -2x + c. Let's find integer points.\n\nFor a=1, b = -2 + c ≤ 4 => c ≤ 6. b ≥ 1 => c ≥ 3. So c ∈ [3,6].\n\nFor a=2, b = -4 + c ≤ 3 => c ≤ 7. b ≥ 1 => c ≥ 5. So c ∈ [5,7].\n\nIntersection: c ∈ [5,6] integer => c = 5 or 6.\n\nCheck c=5: line y = -2x + 5. Points: a=1 => b=3 => (1,3) in S_4; a=2 => b=1 => (2,1) in S_4; a=3 => b=-1 not allowed. So line covers (1,3),(2,1). That's 2 points.\n\nc=6: line y = -2x + 6. a=1 => b=4 => (1,4) in S_4; a=2 => b=2 => (2,2) in S_4; a=3 => b=0 not allowed. So line covers (1,4),(2,2). That's 2 points.\n\nThus slope -2 lines cover at most 2 points.\n\nConsider slope 1/2: y = (1/2)x + c. For integer points, we need x even to get integer y unless c is half-integer. But we can have half-integer c to get integer y for odd x. Let's see.\n\nWe can try lines that pass through two points with difference in x of 2 maybe.\n\nPoints: (1,1),(2,1) can't because same y. (1,2),(3,3)? (3,3) not in S_4. (1,3),(3,4)? (3,4) not in S_4. So not many.\n\nConsider slope -1/2: y = -0.5x + c. For integer x, y is integer if c is half-integer. Let's try to find lines that pass through two points.\n\nTake points (1,4) and (3,2): slope (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. (1,3) and (3,2): slope (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed. So line through (1,3) and (3,2) has slope -1/2. Let's find its equation: slope -1/2, passes through (1,3): y - 3 = -1/2 (x-1) => y = -0.5x + 3.5. So line is y