a from 1 to n), we could assign each line to a column, covering all points in that column. But each column has points (a, b) where b ≤ n+1-a. So column a has n+1-a points. To cover them with a single line, we need a line that passes through all points in that column, i.e., vertical line x=a, which is not sunny. So not allowed.\n\nAlternatively, we could assign each line to a row (b constant). Horizontal lines are not sunny. So not allowed.\n\nThus we need to mix points across rows and columns.\n\nPerhaps we can use lines of the form y = -x + c (slope -1) but that's not sunny. So we need slopes not equal to -1.\n\nOne idea: Use lines with slopes that are reciprocals of integers: slope = -1/(i) for i from 1 to n. For each i, line passes through points (i+1, n-i) maybe? Let's try to design: For i from 1 to n, define line ℓ_i: passes through (i, n+1-i) (boundary) and (i+1, n-i) (also boundary). That line has slope (n-i - (n+1-i)) / ((i+1)-i) = -1, not allowed. So not that.\n\nMaybe use lines that pass through (i, n+1-i) and (i+2, n-1-i) (skip one column). Slope = (n-1-i - (n+1-i)) / ((i+2)-i) = (-2)/2 = -1, still not allowed. So any line connecting two boundary points will have slope -1 because the boundary is a line of slope -1. So we cannot have a line that contains two boundary points. So each sunny line can contain at most one boundary point. So we have n boundary points, and we have n lines, so each line must contain exactly one boundary point. So each line must pass through a unique boundary point and possibly some interior points.\n\nThus we need to assign interior points to lines such that each interior point is covered by some line that also contains a unique boundary point. So we need to partition interior points among the n lines, each line anchored at a distinct boundary point.\n\nNow interior points are those with a+b ≤ n. There are n(n-1)/2 interior points. So each line must cover on average (n-1)/2 interior points. For n=3, interior points are those with a+b ≤ 3: (1,1),(1,2),(2,1). That's 3 interior points. There are 3 boundary points: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). In the solution we found for n=3, each line anchored at a boundary point: ℓ1 anchored at (1,3) and passes through (2,1) (interior). ℓ2 anchored at (2,2) and passes through (1,1) (interior). ℓ3 anchored at (3,1) and passes through (1,2) (interior). Indeed each line contains one boundary point and one interior point. So that works.\n\nFor n=4, we have 4 boundary points: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Interior points: those with sum ≤4: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). That's 6 interior points. We need to assign them to 4 lines anchored at each boundary point. So some lines will need to contain multiple interior points.\n\nLet's try to assign interior points to lines anchored at boundary points such that each line's slope is not 0,∞,-1. For each boundary point B_i = (i, n+1-i). For each interior point P = (a,b) with a+b ≤ n, we can compute the slope of line through B_i and P: m = (b - (n+1-i)) / (a - i). For each interior point, we need to choose a boundary point i such that the slope is not 0,∞,-1. Also the line must be distinct for each i (i.e., each boundary point yields one line). So we need to assign each interior point to a boundary point i, possibly multiple interior points per i, but all interior points assigned to i must lie on the same line (since each i yields a single line). So for each i, we need to find a slope m_i such that all interior points assigned to i lie on the line with that slope through B_i. So we need to partition interior points into groups each lying on a line through a distinct boundary point with a slope not 0,∞,-1.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: can we partition interior points into n groups, each group possibly empty, such that each group together with its associated boundary point are collinear with a slope not 0,∞,-1? Since we have n lines, we can have some lines covering only the boundary point (i.e., no interior points). That's allowe