ts), y=1 (7 points), x+y=8 (7 points), and maybe another line with many points but minimal overlap. Options: vertical x=2 (6 points), horizontal y=2 (6 points), slope -1 line x+y=7 (6 points). We need to choose one that adds many new points while overlapping minimally with the existing three. Let's try x=2 (vertical). Overlaps: x=2 ∩ y=1 = (2,1) (already covered by y=1). x=2 ∩ x=1 none. x=2 ∩ x+y=8 = (2,6) (since 2+6=8). So (2,6) is already covered by x+y=8. So overlapping points: (2,1) and (2,6). So x=2 adds 6 points minus 2 overlaps = 4 new points. So total distinct points covered by 4 lines = 18 + 4 = 22. Let's compute more systematically: 3 lines gave 18 distinct points. Adding x=2 adds 6 points, but we need to subtract overlaps with existing lines: (2,1) is on y=1; (2,6) is on x+y=8. Are there any other overlaps? x=2 and x=1 none. So 2 overlaps. So new distinct points = 6 - 2 = 4. So total distinct = 18 + 4 = 22.\n\nThus with 4 non-sunny lines, we can cover at most 22 distinct points (maybe more if we choose a different line). Let's try horizontal y=2 instead: Overlaps with x=1 at (1,2); with y=1 none; with x+y=8 at (6,2) (since 6+2=8). So overlaps: (1,2) and (6,2). So new distinct = 6 - 2 = 4. So total = 22.\n\nWhat about slope -1 line x+y=7 (6 points): Overlaps with x=1 at (1,6); with y=1 at (6,1); with x+y=8 at (1,7?) Actually x+y=7 intersects x+y=8 at no points (they are parallel). So overlaps: (1,6) and (6,1). So new distinct = 6 - 2 = 4. So total = 22.\n\nThus maximum distinct points covered by 4 non-sunny lines is perhaps 22. Could we do better by picking a different combination? Perhaps we could avoid overlapping with x=1 and y=1 as much as possible. Let's try using x=2, y=2, x+y=8, and x+y=7? But we need exactly 4 lines, not necessarily including both x=1 and y=1. Let's consider all possible sets of 4 non-sunny lines. We want to maximize distinct coverage. The maximum distinct coverage is achieved by selecting lines that each have many points and minimal overlaps. The three lines x=1, y=1, x+y=n+1 each have n points and overlap pairwise at distinct points. That's already 3n - 3 distinct points. Adding a fourth line will inevitably overlap with at least one of these three lines at some points. The maximum new points contributed by the fourth line is its total points minus the number of overlaps. The total points for a vertical/horizontal line x=c (c>1) is n+1-c ≤ n-1. For c=2, it's n-1 = 6 points. Overlaps with x=1: none. Overlaps with y=1: (c,1) = (2,1). Overlaps with x+y=n+1: (c, n+1-c) = (2,6). So two overlaps. So new distinct = (n-1) - 2 = n-3 = 4 for n=7. So total distinct = 3n - 3 + (n-3) = 4n - 6 = 4*7 - 6 = 28 - 6 = 22. So indeed 22 distinct points. Could we pick a line with more points? The only line with n points is x=1, y=1, x+y=n+1. So the fourth line must be one of the others with at most n-1 points. So maximum distinct points = 3n - 3 + (n-1) - (minimum overlaps). Minimum overlaps for a line with n-1 points is at least 2 (since it intersects each of the three lines at at most one point each, but it may intersect two of them). Is it possible to intersect only one of the three lines? Let's check: a vertical line x=c (c>1) intersects y=1 at (c,1) and x+y=n+1 at (c, n+1-c). So it intersects both y=1 and x+y=n+1, unless c = n+1-c (i.e., c = (n+1)/2) which for n odd gives intersection at same point? Actually if c = (n+1)/2, then (c, n+1-c) = (c,c). So the line x=c intersects x+y=n+1 at (c,c). So still an intersection. So vertical line intersects both y=1 and x+y=n+1, unless c=1 (which we already have). So at least 2 overlaps. Similarly, a horizontal line y=c (c>1) intersects x=1 at (1,c) and x+y=n+1 at (n+1-c,c). So at least 2 overlaps. A slope -1 line x+y=c (c < n+1) intersects x=1 at (1,c-1) and y=1 at (c-1,1). So again at least 2 overlaps. So any fourth line will have at least 2 overlaps with the three maximal lines. So new distinct points ≤ (n-1) - 2 = n-3. So total distinct ≤ 3n - 3 + (n-3) = 4n - 6. 