t ≥ 1 => s + t ≤ n-1. And also x≥1 automatically. So same condition. Similarly H_t and D_s intersect at (x,y) = (n+1-s - (1+t), 1+t) = (n - s - t, 1+t). Condition x≥1 => n - s - t ≥ 1 => s + t ≤ n-1. So any intersection between lines from different families is in S_n if the sum of their indices ≤ n-1.\n\nThus the triple {V_t, H_t, D_t} has each pair intersecting at points (1+t,1+t), (1+t,n-t), (n-t,1+t). Indeed these are the three intersections. Those three points are distinct and are in S_n because t + t = 2t ≤ n-1? Actually for t up to floor((n-1)/2) maybe? Let's check: For V_t and H_t, intersection is (1+t,1+t). Condition 2t ≤ n-1 => t ≤ (n-1)/2. But the solution says t runs from 0 to n-1. However for t > floor((n-1)/2), the intersection (1+t,1+t) would have sum 2+2t, which is > n+1? Let's compute: (1+t)+(1+t) = 2+2t. Condition ≤ n+1 => 2t ≤ n-1 => t ≤ (n-1)/2. So for t > floor((n-1)/2), the intersection point (1+t,1+t) is not in S_n. But the solution says \"Inside a fixed triple {V_t, H_t, D_t} the three lines intersect pairwise in the three distinct points (1+t,1+t), (1+t,n-t), (n-t,1+t).\" However, those points might not all be in S_n for all t. Actually (1+t,n-t) sum = 1+t + n-t = n+1, which is allowed (equal to n+1). So (1+t,n-t) is always in S_n for t from 0 to n-1 because n-t ≥ 1. Similarly (n-t,1+t) sum = n+1. So those two points are always in S_n. The point (1+t,1+t) is only in S_n for t ≤ floor((n-1)/2). For t larger, it's outside S_n. However, the solution's counting argument for the triple contributions might assume that the three intersections are counted as overlapping points that have been already counted when adding the lines. They say each triple contributes 3(n-t)-3 new points (the three intersections have already been counted). But if one of the intersections is not in S_n, then it's not counted at all, so the overlap count might be different. However, they might be counting only the points of S_n that are on the lines. For t > floor((n-1)/2), 