1,8) already covered (a=1). (2,7) not yet covered (a=2,b=7). (3,6) not covered. (4,5) not covered. (5,4) not covered. (6,3) not covered. (7,2) not covered. (8,1) already covered (b=1). So diagonal adds 6 new points: (2,7),(3,6),(4,5),(5,4),(6,3),(7,2). So total distinct points now = 15 + 6 = 21.\n\n- x=2 points: (2,1)-(2,7). Among these, (2,1) already covered (b=1). (2,2) not covered yet. (2,3) not covered. (2,4) not covered. (2,5) not covered. (2,6) not covered. (2,7) already covered (diagonal). So x=2 adds points (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(2,5),(2,6). That's 5 new points. So total distinct points = 21 + 5 = 26.\n\nThus after 4 non-sunny lines, we have covered 26 points out of 36, leaving 10 points uncovered. We have k=4 sunny lines left (since total lines = n = 8, k = 4). So we need to cover 10 points with 4 sunny lines, each covering at most maybe 3 points? For n=8, slope 1 lines can have up to floor((n+1)/2) = floor(9/2) = 4 points. But the uncovered points might not be arranged to allow 4 points per line. Let's list the uncovered points after these 4 non-sunny lines.\n\nUncovered points are those not in the union above. Let's list all points (a,b) with a,b≥1, a+b ≤9 (since n+1=9). We have total 36 points. Let's list them systematically:\n\nSum = 2: (1,1) covered.\nSum = 3: (1,2),(2,1) covered (both covered).\nSum = 4: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). (1,3) covered (x=1). (2,2) uncovered? Not covered yet. (3,1) covered (y=1). So (2,2) uncovered.\n\nSum = 5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). (1,4) covered (x=1). (2,3) uncovered. (3,2) uncovered? Not covered yet. (4,1) covered (y=1). So (2,3),(3,2) uncovered.\n\nSum = 6: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). (1,5) covered (x=1). (2,4) uncovered. (3,3) uncovered? Not covered. (4,2) uncovered? Not covered. (5,1) covered (y=1). So (2,4),(3,3),(4,2) uncovered.\n\nSum = 7: (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1). (1,6) covered (x=1). (2,5) uncovered. (3,4) uncovered? Not covered. (4,3) uncovered? Not covered. (5,2) uncovered? Not covered. (6,1) covered (y=1). 