7\\le n\\le9\\).)\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n*The detailed proof of points\u202f1\u20136 is given below; points\u202f7\u20138 are presented as a rigorous partial result.*\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny vs. non\u2011sunny.**  \n   A line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to one of the three directions\n   \\[\n   \\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n   \\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n   \\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n   \\]\n   All other lines are called *sunny*.\n\n3. **Maximum number of points covered by \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(a,b,c\\ge0\\) with \\(a+b+c=m\\); we take \\(a\\) vertical, \\(b\\) horizontal and \\(c\\) diagonal lines.\n   The vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=a\\) contain respectively \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n-a+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); similarly for the horizontal and diagonal families.  \n   Adding these lengths and subtracting the pairwise intersections (each intersection is a single point of \\(S_n\\)) gives\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\#\\text{covered}\n   &=\\bigl[n+(n-1)+\\dots+(n-a+1)\\bigr]\n     +\\bigl[n+(n-1)+\\dots+(n-b+1)\\bigr] \\\\\n   &\\qquad+\\bigl[n+(n-1)+\\dots+(n-c+1)\\bigr]\n     -\\bigl[\\tbinom a2+\\tbinom b2+\\tbinom c2\\bigr] .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the arithmetic yields\n   \\[\n   f(m)=mn-\\frac{m(m-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The right\u2011hand side depends only on \\(m\\), not on the distribution \\((a,b,c)\\); consequently (1) is the *maximum* possible number of points that can be covered by any choice of \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n4. **Uncovered points when exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines are used.**  \n   In a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we have \\(m=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Using (1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   |S_n|-f(m)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\Bigl[(n-k)n-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}2\\Bigr]\n            =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Thus any such configuration must leave at least \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points that are not on a non\u2011sunny line.\n\n5. **A sunny line can contain at most three uncovered points.**  \n   Let \\(P\\) be a point of \\(S_n\\) that is not on any non\u2011sunny line.  \n   If a sunny line \\(L\\) contained four such points, then among those four points two would have the same \\(x\\)\u2013coordinate, the same \\(y\\)\u2013coordinate or the same sum \\(x+y\\) (pigeon\u2011hole principle applied to the three possible \u201ccoordinates\u201d).  The line through that pair would be vertical, horizontal or diagonal, contradicting the fact that \\(L\\) is sunny.  Hence a sunny line can contain at most three points that are not already covered by a non\u2011sunny line.\n\n6. **Upper bound \\(k\\le3\\).**  \n   From (2) the number of points that must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at least \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \n   By the observation of step\u202f5 each sunny line can contribute at most three new points, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(3k\\) new points.  Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le3k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k^2-5k\\le0,\n   \\]\n   whence \\(0\\le k\\le3\\).  No configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) exists for any \\(n\\).\n\n7. **The smallest case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) we have \\(|S_3|=6\\).  \n   If \\(k=2\\) then \\(m=1\\); by (1) a single non\u2011sunny line covers at most three points, leaving three points uncovered.  Any two of those three points share either the same \\(x\\), the same \\(y\\) or the same sum, so every line through a pair is non\u2011sunny.  Hence the three uncovered points cannot be covered by the two sunny lines, and \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  The same reasoning shows that \\(k=3\\) is impossible.  \n\n   *Existence for \\(k=0,1\\).*  \n   \u2013 \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) cover all six points.  \n   \u2013 \\(k=1\\): the two vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) cover five points; the remaining point \\((3,1)\\) is covered by the sunny line through \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) (slope \\(-\\frac12\\)).  \n\n   Thus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are exactly \\(k=0,1\\).\n\n8. **Partial constructions for larger \\(n\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) for every \\(n\\).*  \n   \u2013 \\(k=0\\): the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   \u2013 \\(k=1\\): the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).\n\n   *\\(k=2\\) for all \\(n\\ge7\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  By (1) they cover all points of \\(S_n\\) except the three points\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n   The line through \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) has slope \\(-\\frac1{\\,n-2\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny and covers the two points \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  The remaining point \\((n-1,1)\\) is covered by the sunny line through it and \\((1,n-1)\\); this line has slope \\(-\\frac{n-2}{\\,n-2\\,}=-1\\) only when \\(n=3\\), which does not occur here, so the line is sunny.  Thus the two sunny lines together with the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\) for all \\(n\\ge10\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The uncovered set is\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1)\\},\n   \\]\n   which has six points as required by (2).  The following three sunny lines cover them:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: \\text{through }(n-2,3)\\text{ and }(n,1), &\n   \\text{slope }-\\frac{2}{\\,n-2\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1;\\\\\n   L_2 &: \\text{through }(n-2,2)\\text{ and }(n-1,1), &\n   \\text{slope }-\\frac{1}{\\,n-3\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1;\\\\\n   L_3 &: \\text{through }(n-2,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2), &\n   \\text{slope }1\\neq0,\\infty,-1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Each line is sunny, the three lines are distinct, and together they cover all six points of \\(U\\).  Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines yields a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly three sunny lines covering all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   The constructions above prove that\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   k=2 \\text{ is possible for every } n\\ge7,\\\\[2pt]\n   k=3 \\text{ is possible for every } n\\ge10.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   The cases \\(4\\le n\\le6\\) for \\(k=2\\) and \\(7\\le n\\le9\\) for \\(k=3\\) remain unsettled in this write\u2011up.  \n\n--- \n\n**Conclusion.**  \nWe have shown rigorously that \\(k\\) can never exceed\u202f3, that for the minimal size \\(n=3\\) only \\(k=0,1\\) occur, and that \\(k=0,1\\) are always attainable.  Moreover we have explicit constructions showing that \\(k=2\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge7\\) and \\(k=3\\) for all \\(n\\ge10\\).  Determining the exact admissible values of \\(k\\) for the remaining intermediate values of \\(n\\) is left as an open (but clearly approachable) problem."
