st three points of P may be false. But maybe they consider that the points in P are uncovered by non-sunny lines, which means they must have distinct x, y, and sum? Actually uncovered points are those not lying on any vertical, horizontal, or diagonal line that is used among the non-sunny lines. But the non-sunny lines used are some selection of vertical/horizontal/diagonal lines. The uncovered points are those not covered by those lines. So they could be any points that are not on those lines. So they could have same x coordinate as a vertical line not used? Wait, vertical lines used are some subset of possible x values. If we have m non-sunny lines, they could be vertical lines at some x-values, horizontal lines at some y-values, diagonal lines at some sums. The uncovered points are those that avoid those x-values, y-values, sums. So each uncovered point has x not among the chosen vertical lines' x-values, y not among chosen horizontal lines' y-values, and sum not among chosen diagonal lines' sums. So each uncovered point has a unique x among the set of x-values not used, similarly for y and sum. However, there could be two uncovered points sharing the same x-coordinate if that x-coordinate is not used by any vertical line. But if there are two uncovered points sharing same x, then that x is not used by any vertical line, but they share x, so they are both uncovered. That is allowed. But then a sunny line passing through them would be vertical? Actually a line through two points with same x-coordinate is vertical, which is non-sunny. So a sunny line cannot contain two points with same x. Similarly for same y or same sum. So indeed any sunny line cannot contain two points that share x, y, or sum. So the claim that any sunny line can contain at most three points of P is based on the pigeonhole principle: among four points, two must share one of the three coordinates (x, y, sum). But as we argued, it's not guaranteed that among any four points there must be two shari