constant or y=constant. So we need slanted lines.\n\nPotential lines:\n\n- Slope 1: lines of form y = x + c. For points in P, we have y - x values: (7,1): -6; (8,2): -6; (9,3): -6 (not in P); (10,4): -6 (not in P). So points (7,1) and (8,2) share same y-x = -6, so they lie on line y = x - 6. That's a sunny line (slope 1). It also includes (9,3) not in P. So line L1: y = x - 6 covers (7,1),(8,2). Also (9,3) not needed.\n\n- Similarly, points (7,2) and (8,3) share y - x = -5, line y = x - 5 covers (7,2),(8,3). Also (9,4) not in P.\n\n- Points (7,3) and (8,4) not in P. So we have two lines covering 2 points each.\n\n- Points (7,4) and (8,5) not in P. So (7,4) is alone.\n\n- Points (9,1) and (10,2) not in P (10,2 sum=12 > 11). So (9,1) alone.\n\n- Points (9,2) and (10,3) not in P. So (9,2) alone.\n\n- Points (8,1) and (9,2) share y - x = -7? (8,1): -7; (9,2): -7. So line y = x - 7 covers (8,1),(9,2). Also (10,3) not in P.\n\n- Points (8,2) already covered by L1.\n\n- Points (8,3) covered by L2.\n\n- Points (7,1) covered by L1.\n\n- Points (7,2) covered by L2.\n\n- Points (7,3) not covered yet.\n\n- Points (7,4) not covered.\n\n- Points (8,1) covered by L3.\n\n- Points (9,1) not covered.\n\n- Points (9,2) covered by L3.\n\n- Points (10,1) not covered.\n\nSo far we have three lines covering: L1: (7,1),(8,2); L2: (7,2),(8,3); L3: (8,1),(9,2). That's 6 points covered. Remaining points: (7,3),(7,4),(9,1),(10,1). That's 4 points left. We have one more sunny line allowed (since we need exactly 4 sunny lines). Can we cover these 4 points with a single sunny line? They need to be collinear with slope not 0, ∞, -1. Let's see if any line passes through all four. Likely not. But maybe we can adjust the selection of lines to cover more points per line.\n\nAlternatively, we could use lines with slope 2: For points (7,1) and (9,2) slope (2-1)/(9-7)=0.5, not 2. Let's compute slopes for various pairs.\n\nBetter approach: Use lines that go through three points. For example, points (7,1),(8,2),(9,3) but (9,3) not in P. So not possible. Points (7,2),(8,3),(9,4) not in P. Points (7,3),(8,4) not in P. So no triple with slope 1.\n\nWhat about slope 2? Points (7,1),(8,3) slope (3-1)/(8-7)=2. Then also (9,5) not in P. So line covers (7,1),(8,3). That's two points.\n\nSlope 1/2: Points (7,1),(9,2) slope 0.5, also (11,3) not in P. So covers (7,1),(9,2). Already covered by L3? L3 is (8,1),(9,2) slope 1? Actually L3 slope (2-1)/(9-8)=1, sorry L3 is slope 1 as well? Wait L3: (8,1) and (9,2) slope (2-1)/(9-8)=1, yes slope 1. So (9,2) already covered.\n\nWhat about slope -2? (7,4) and (8,2) slope (2-4)/(8-7) = -2, okay. Also (9,0) not in P. So line covers (7,4),(8,2). But (8,2) already covered by L1. So that line would cover (7,4) and (8,2). Could we use a line that covers (7,4),(8,2),(9,0) not needed. So we could cover (7,4) with a line that also covers (8,2) but (8,2) already covered, but that's okay. So we could replace L1 with a line covering (7,1),(8,2),(9,3) not needed. Actually we need to cover (7,4) and maybe (9,1) together? Let's see slope between (7,4) and (9,1) = (1-4)/(9-7) = -3/2 = -1.5, which is not -1, so it's sunny. Does it also pass through (8,2.5) not integer. So line covers only those two points.\n\nAlternatively, we could try to cover (9,1) and (10,1) with a line? That would be horizontal (slope 0), not allowed. So cannot cover both (9,1) and (10,1) with a sunny line. So they must be covered separately or with other points.\n\nThus covering all 10 points with 4 sunny lines seems challenging. But maybe there is a different selection of non-sunny lines that leaves a different uncovered set that can be covered by 4 sunny lines.\n\nThe solution's bound k ≤ 3 might be correct. However, the reasoning for the bound uses a flawed claim about sunny lines covering at most 3 points of P. But the bound may still hold due to other constraints. Let's examine the bound more carefully.\n\nWe have uncovered points count = k(k+1)/2. The sunny lines are k lines