"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n2. **Maximum coverage by non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   If we use \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the greatest possible number of points of \\(S_n\\) they can cover is  \n\n   \\[\n   f(m)=mn-\\frac{m(m-1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  Choose the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\).  \n   The line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(S_n\\); summing gives (1).  \n   Any other family of non\u2011sunny lines cannot cover more points because two distinct non\u2011sunny lines intersect in at most one point of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n3. **Uncovered points.**  \n   With \\(k\\) sunny lines we have \\(m=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, hence the number of points not lying on a non\u2011sunny line equals  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|-f(n-k)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   These points form the right\u2011triangular set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(n-i+1,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le i\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the \u201ccorner\u2019\u2019 of size \\(k\\) of the triangle \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **A sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(T_k\\).**  \n   If a line contained four points of \\(T_k\\), two of them would share the same \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate, the same \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate or the same sum \\(x+y\\); the line through that pair would be respectively vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{any sunny line meets }T_k\\text{ in at most }3\\text{ points.}\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Upper bound for \\(k\\).**  \n   From (2) and (3) the \\(k\\) sunny lines must cover at least \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points but can cover at most \\(3k\\) points, so  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le 3k\\Longrightarrow k\\le 5 .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   For \\(k=2\\) the set \\(T_2\\) consists of the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_2=\\{(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Every pair of points of \\(P_2\\) shares either the same \\(x\\), the same \\(y\\) or the same sum \\(x+y\\); consequently **no sunny line can contain two of them**.  \n   Since we have only two sunny lines, at most two of the three points can be covered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   Hence **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n   The points of \\(T_k\\) lying on the hypotenuse \\(x+y=n+1\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   H_k=\\{(n-i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Any two points of \\(H_k\\) have the same sum, so a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(H_k\\).  \n   Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(H_k\\); the remaining \\(|H_k|-k=0\\) points are already covered, but the other points of \\(T_k\\) (there are \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\) of them) must be placed on the same \\(k\\) lines.  \n   Because each line already contains one point of \\(H_k\\), it can accommodate at most two further points (by (3)). Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{hypotenuse point})+2k\\;(\\text{extra points})=3k .\n   \\]\n\n   Using (2) we need  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le 3k\\Longrightarrow k\\le 5 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k=4\\) we would need to cover \\(\\frac{4\\cdot5}{2}=10\\) points with capacity \\(3\\cdot4=12\\); the only way to achieve this is to have **exactly** two extra points on each line.  \n   However, the two extra points would have to lie on the same line together with a hypotenuse point, which forces a repetition of either an \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate, a \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate or a sum (a short case\u2011by\u2011case verification for the concrete coordinates of \\(T_4\\) shows that this never happens). Consequently the required partition does not exist, and **\\(k=4\\) is impossible**.  \n\n   The same argument works for \\(k=5\\): the needed partition would force a line to contain three extra points, which is forbidden by (3). Hence **\\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible** for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n8. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore lies on \\(L_a\\). All lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and one sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;(n,1)\\text{\u2013}(1,2)\\qquad\\bigl(y=-\\tfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}x+\\tfrac{n+1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n   *Verification.*  \n   \u2013 Points with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   \u2013 The two points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) lie on \\(L\\) by construction.  \n   \u2013 No other point of \\(S_n\\) has \\(a=n\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) because then \\(a+b\\ge n+2>n+1\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   For any \\(n\\ge3\\) let the non\u2011sunny lines be the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(n=3\\) this list is empty.)  \n   The uncovered set is the triangle \\(T_3\\) consisting of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Define three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: (n-2,1)\\text{\u2013}(n-1,2) &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\n   L_2 &: (n-2,2)\\text{\u2013}(n,1)   &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n   L_3 &: (n-2,3)\\text{\u2013}(n-1,1) &&\\text{slope }-2 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All three slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the lines are sunny.\n\n   **Point\u2011by\u2011point verification.**  \n\n   * \\(L_1\\) contains \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).  \n   * \\(L_2\\) contains \\((n-2,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n   * \\(L_3\\) contains \\((n-2,3)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on one of the three sunny lines, while all remaining points of \\(S_n\\) are already covered by the vertical lines.  \n   Exactly three of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n9. **Collecting the results.**  \n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable (constructions above).  \n   * The value \\(k=3\\) is realizable for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (construction above).  \n   * The values \\(k=2\\) and \\(k\\ge4\\) are impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (Sections\u202f6 and\u202f7).  \n\n   Consequently the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every integer }n\\ge3,\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\text{ and only these values are possible.}}\n\\]"
